<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warrior of Hirschlanden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Krieger von Hirschlanden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Warrior of Hirschlanden is a sandstone statue of a warrior, the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> life-size anthropomorphic statue north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. A work of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a>, probably of the 6th century BCE, it was found in 1963 beside a low barrow with 16 burials at Hirschlanden, now part of Ditzingen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and is supposed to have stood on its top. Its preserved height is 1.50 m, the feet having been broken off. The figure wears a torc, a belt with a late Hallstatt dagger and a pointed hat, and its sketchy face has led to speculation that it wears a mask. It is now in the Württembergisches Landesmuseum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, with a copy at the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warrior-of-hirschlanden.jpg" alt="Warrior of Hirschlanden" width="330" height="751" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florian Hoffmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krieger_Hirschlanden_Vs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The figure's pointed hat may have been made of birchbark, like the real hat found in the princely grave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hochdorf-chieftains-grave">Hochdorf</a>. Its legs are modelled with some realism, but the upper body is schematic. Masks of the kind it may wear are known from burials at Klein-Klein in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and Trebeništa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, and much earlier from the shaft graves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> of about 1500 BCE. Heavy weathering suggests that the statue stood in the open for a long time before it was buried.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Other human figures of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> have been found at Rottenburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, Stammheim and Stockach in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, but they are far more stylised, more carving than statue. Greek influence from the kouroi has been discussed: black-figure vessels from fortified settlements of the period show trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, probably along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and through the Greek colony of Massilia. Much closer in style are the far more elaborate statues of Capestrano in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, of about 550 BCE, and of Casale Marittimo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, from the middle of the 7th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The statue came to light when the barrow was excavated in 1963. The mound was ringed by a stone circle and a dry stone wall, and its burials range from the very start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, Hallstatt D1 of about 600 BCE, to the beginning of the La Tène period, about 450 BCE. In the La Tène period that followed, statues of people remained very rare; examples include the finds from Glauberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, the Holzgerlingen figure, the Mšecké Žehrovice Head in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and the sculptures of Roquepertuse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Warrior of Hirschlanden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earliest monumental stone sculptures, the <b>Warrior of Hirschlanden</b> and the “Glauberg Prince” from Germany, date to the 6th and 5th centuries BCE, though it is far from clear that they show deities.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A few pieces, the Strettweg Cult Wagon, the <b>Warrior of Hirschlanden</b> and the bronze couch held up by “unicyclists” from the Hochdorf Chieftain’s Grave, are each one of a kind.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
